	<h1 class="page-title">Research</h1>
	<p class="page-lede">Who really decides in energy governance, with what means, and how that asymmetry is legitimised.</p>
	<div class="page-body recherche-page">

<p>My research object is the gap between formally attributed authority and the actual capacity to act in energy governance: who really decides, with what means, and how that asymmetry is legitimised. The thread runs from the metropolis to the international stage, from grid concessions to regional interconnections, and cuts across my academic articles, think tank notes and journalistic work.</p>

<hr class="section-sep" />

<h2>Research areas</h2>
<ul>
<li>Capacity asymmetry in energy governance: concessions, regulation, planning.</li>
<li>Energy, territories and metropolises.</li>
<li>Energy geopolitics: oil and OPEC+, LNG, chokepoints, semiconductors.</li>
<li>Energy transitions in Southeast Asia; Taiwan and recognition.</li>
<li>African power systems and regional integration (Central Africa).</li>
<li>Decentralisation and energy justice.</li>
</ul>

<hr class="section-sep" />

<h2>Peer-reviewed publications</h2>
<ul>
<li>"La fenêtre de rente&nbsp;: la sortie émirienne de l'OPEP+ comme aboutissement", <em>La Revue de l'Énergie</em>, no.&nbsp;685 (2026/4), pp.&nbsp;48-62. <a href="https://stm.cairn.info/revue-revue-de-l-energie-2026-4-page-48?lang=fr" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Read on Cairn&nbsp;→</a></li>
<li>"Les transitions énergétiques en Asie du Sud-Est", chapter co-written with Gauthier Mouton, in <em>L'Asie du Sud-Est 2027</em>, IRASEC, ed. J. Jammes. Forthcoming.</li>
<li>Several articles under review in French and international peer-reviewed journals.</li>
</ul>

